    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for the breakdown. regarding the live load, what kind of safety factor do you usually recommend for residential builds in bc? also, are there specific things i should look out for that might cause unexpected structural stress or failure over time?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In BC, it's common to use a safety factor of 1.4 to 1.6 for live loads on residential decks. This means that if you're using a 40 psf live load, you'd multiply it by 1.4 or 1.6 to get the total load that the beam can handle. \n\nHowever, this is just a general guideline, and you should always check the specific requirements of the BC Building Code and any local regulations. \n\nAs for unexpected structural stress or failure, there are a few things to watch out for. One common issue is the weight of heavy furniture or appliances, which can put additional stress on the deck. You should also consider the weight of snow or ice in the winter, which can be a major factor in BC's climate. \n\nAnother thing to keep an eye on is the condition of the deck over time. As the cedar boards and joists weather and degrade, they can become weaker and more prone to failure. You should also make sure that the deck is properly anchored to the house and that the connections are secure. \n\nIt's also worth noting that the type of fasteners used can affect the structural integrity of the deck. For example, using galvanized or stainless",
      "source": "target",
